{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:outside","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:c300bc7c01ff7b3d917bf155dc1ac79af5f04a3c0a9412c216c3f48fc325c29d","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.65},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.95},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.35}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"FIRST, a mechanical restatement of the anchor, which the brief's framing invites getting wrong: US 10y TIPS ~2.60% (FRED DFII10, Oct 30 2026) is NOT the G7 GDP-weighted level. At approximate market-FX GDP weights (US ~0.55, Japan ~0.10, Germany ~0.09, UK ~0.07, France ~0.06, Italy ~0.05, Canada ~0.05) and plausible current component real 10y yields (US 2.6, JP ~0.6, DE ~1.1, UK ~1.6, FR ~1.4, IT ~2.0, CA ~1.9), the GDP-weighted starting level is ~1.95-2.00%. Reference class (decade averages of G7 real 10y yields): 1970s negative to ~0% (real rates negative for much of the decade per RBA Bulletin Oct 1991); 1980s ~+4 to +5% (Macrobond: US real 10y ~5% post-inflation, best decade in its series); 1990s ~+3 to +3.5%; 2000s ~+2.0%; 2010s ~+0.1 to +0.3% GDP-weighted (US 10y TIPS ~0.2% average 2009-2021 per QIC; US median real yield 2.7% for 1962-2007 vs 0.4% post-2008 per Renaissance — figures not independently verified); 2020-2025 ~+0.4% (negative 2020-21, +1.8-2.2% 2023-25). Unconditional range across six decades: roughly -1% to +5%. Base rate of a decade-average real yield staying near its starting level: LOW — 4 of 5 decade transitions moved >=100bp, mean absolute decade-over-decade change ~200bp. But the long-run 1960-2025 G7 GDP-weighted mean is ~1.8-2.2%, so the starting level ~2.0% already SITS on the reversion target. Mean reversion therefore implies near-zero central drift with very wide bands — the opposite of what the 2.60% framing suggests. Decomposition r* + real term premium - repression wedge: (i) r*: G7 GDP-weighted ~0.8-1.0% on HLW-style estimates (US 0.7-1.5, euro area/Japan 0.2-0.8). Boomer dissaving and physical/grid capex demand push up; precautionary saving under labour-income uncertainty, cognitive-compression income loss, and the Chinese/OECD savings gluts push down. 2A itself concedes a frictionless r* may EASE. Central contribution +1.0, 1-sigma ~0.45. (ii) Real term premium: ~+0.5 to +1.0pp pre-2008, ~-0.5pp under QE 2012-2020, +0.5 to +1.0 in 2023-26. Rising rollover volumes, eroding sovereign convenience yields, and levered basis-trade absorption as a fragile marginal buyer push up; the historical debt-to-real-yield elasticity is weak (Japan 1990-2020: debt 65%->250% of GDP while real yields FELL; US 1945-1980: debt falling while real yields negative), so I allow only ~1-3bp per pp of debt/GDP: +25pp x 2bp = +50bp. Central contribution +0.9, 1-sigma ~0.60. (iii) Repression wedge: a genuine policy branch, not a tail. Base rate of at least one G7 sovereign under de facto yield management in a post-1945 5-yr window is very high (1945-1980 sustained G7 repression; Japan YCC 2016-2024; ECB PEPP/TPI spread management). GDP-weighted magnitude is what matters: ~45% x ~100bp (materially binding) + ~30% x ~25bp (mild) => expected -0.55pp, central -0.5, 1-sigma ~0.45. Central = 1.0 + 0.9 - 0.5 = 1.4%; I weight this 40% against the 60%-weighted base-rate/mean-reversion anchor of ~2.0-2.1% to land P50 = 1.95%. VARIANCE ATTRIBUTION: real term premium ~55%, r* ~25%, repression wedge ~20%. The term premium carries most of it, which is exactly what the 2A (r - g) claim is about: that claim is fiscal arithmetic and premium, not an r* claim, and my decomposition keeps r* nearly flat while letting the premium and the wedge fight. Bands respect the decade base rate: ~33% of historical decades were below +1.0% (1970s, 2010s) and ~33% above +3.0% (1980s, 1990s), so P10 = 0.65, P90 = 3.35.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.034Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 harmonized unemployment annual average prints <=4.6% in BOTH 2027 and 2028 (OECD Main Economic Indicators / Labour Force Statistics), with no member-weighted recession quarter in either year.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my recession-exposure argument for CQ1's first leg: that marginal moves 0.42 -> ~0.60 and CQ1 moves 9 -> ~15. It also shifts ~3pp of CQ6 mass from B to A by raising the labour-input path, and raises my CQ3 P50 by ~0.15pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE calendar-year average >=120 in any two consecutive years within 2027-2030 (versus ~59-64 at end-2025).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my \\\"starting from a volatility trough, a 10-yr average of 105 needs an implausible 4-5 years above 130\\\" arithmetic: CQ5 conjunct A moves 0.25 -> ~0.50 and CQ5 moves 17 -> ~30. It also pushes CQ4's P90 up ~40bp via the real term premium and adds ~4pp to CQ6-C at B's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS) annual average >=2.0% in three consecutive years, 2029-2031.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Destroys the Japan-post-1995 analogue that is my entire basis for CQ2's second leg: that marginal moves 0.66 -> ~0.25 and CQ2 collapses 18 -> ~8. It simultaneously removes the Chinese goods-deflation drag on OECD core, which raises P(OECD core >=2.7%) and adds ~3pp to CQ6-C.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (10y nominal benchmark minus 10y inflation swap / breakeven, GDP-weighted at market FX) averages >=3.2% in BOTH 2031 and 2032.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my mean-reversion-to-~2.0% anchor and says the fiscal-arithmetic / term-premium branch has beaten the repression branch: CQ4 moves to P10 ~1.30 / P50 ~2.80 / P90 ~4.00. It raises CQ5 conjunct A, adds ~4pp to CQ6-C and removes ~5pp from CQ6-D (high real yields are inconsistent with a savings-glut demand shortfall).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"At least two G7 sovereigns simultaneously operate an explicit yield cap, an announced yield-curve-control programme covering >=10y maturities, or a new statutory bank/insurer captive-holding or reserve mandate for domestic government debt.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms the repression branch I priced at ~45% and moves my expected wedge from -0.50pp to ~-1.20pp: CQ4 moves to P10 ~0.20 / P50 ~1.30 / P90 ~2.60. It lowers CQ5 conjunct A (managed curves suppress MOVE) taking CQ5 to ~11, and shifts ~4pp of CQ6 mass from C into B and D, since repression trades a term-premium spike for a slower-growth, administered-rate equilibrium.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 outside seat, effort 'rstar'."}}